WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.700
Navigation to https://example.test/account?tab=billing#history

2
00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:16.110
A passive observer sits on the network path. Illustration, not a capture.

3
00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:23.380
Plain DNS: the question for example.test is unencrypted.

4
00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:30.650
Visibility depends on DNS transport and observer position.

5
00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:36.430
Destination IP, port 443, timing and volume are visible.

6
00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:42.860
An IP address does not necessarily identify one unique website.

7
00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:49.620
ClientHello is plaintext. No ECH: the SNI names example.test.

8
00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:56.070
ServerHello: keys agreed. The rest of the handshake is encrypted.

9
00:00:56.070 --> 00:01:01.700
TLS 1.3: the server certificate is inside the encrypted handshake.

10
00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:06.770
The observer sees a record length, not the certificate.

11
00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:10.530
GET /account?tab=billing, headers, cookies, body: encrypted.

12
00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:18.040
Record sizes and timing leak, but not the exact content by themselves.

13
00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:27.630
Browser and TLS endpoint both hold the plaintext. The server may log it.

14
00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:32.790
The fragment #history never left the browser.

15
00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:38.440
Encrypted content can still travel with visible metadata.

16
00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:48.330
Encrypted DNS hides the question, not the IP. ECH hides the SNI, not everything.
